WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.167
MAX SALTONSTALL: Millions
of people in New York City

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:03.750
means billions of
events every day.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.333
Join us as we talk with New York
City Cyber Command about what

00:00:06.333 --> 00:00:08.550
these events are, why it's
important to track them,

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.470
and how they built
a scalable system

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.373
to protect all of the people in
New York City on this episode

00:00:13.373 --> 00:00:14.040
of "Stack Chat."

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.613
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:19.030
Thanks for joining
us today, Noam.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.488
Can you tell us a little bit
about what New York City Cyber

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:22.120
Command does?

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:22.380
NOAM DOROGOYER: Sure.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.350
Thanks for having us.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.100
So New York City Cyber
Command is an organization

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.260
that is responsible for
preventing, detecting,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.970
responding, and
recovering to any threats

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.500
to our city systems.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.490
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's
a pretty big mandate.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.948
And you've got a
lot of information

00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:35.260
coming in every day.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.200
Can you tell me how
you built a pipeline

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.697
to process all of that data?

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:40.530
NOAM DOROGOYER: We have
tons of log sources.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.300
I think we're
approaching 20 now,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.920
and we'll have more by the day.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.830
So we have on cloud and on prem.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.270
And the way we
split things up is

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:53.430
that, for on prem log sources,
we use the syslog-ng protocol.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.000
And we use it to enrich
all logs with metadata,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.480
whether it be IPs, or
timestamps, and so on.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.880
For on-cloud, we use
Google's Cloud Functions

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.510
to perform a similar function.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.440
So once we get that
data, we can output

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.630
it using Google's Pub/Sub.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.710
And we have an application
called Flow Control

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.720
that inputs all this data.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.010
And it runs on Google's
Data Flow and Apache Beam

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:24.810
in order to remove all corrupt,
meaningless, or incomplete data

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.300
so further down the pipeline,
we don't have to handle it,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.260
and we don't have to
distract from the parsers.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.710
So once that's complete,
we, once again,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.120
use Google's Pub/Sub to
now take all our logs

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.760
into our main piece of
logic, which is the parsers.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.490
So for every single log, we
have a different set of code,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.180
whether it's separated by
a bunch of mumbo jumbo,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.890
or comma separated, and so on.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.760
Now we can make it into
meaningful data and something

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.280
that's queriable by
our security analysts.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.890
And the best place for
that would be BigQuery.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.820
Once it's in BigQuery, we can
start taking all our data,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.130
and applying machine
learning algorithms,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.310
outputting it into dashboards,
or just having something

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.300
as simple as having our security
analysts query it and find

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.050
something dangerous or
maybe a hacking attempt.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.070
MAX SALTONSTALL: So if you could
go back and tell past Noam what

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.900
to do from the
beginning, what lesson

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.450
would you give yourself to
make this whole process easier?

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.150
NOAM DOROGOYER: Google
Pub/Sub is unordered.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.240
And as a result, that
made it so that we

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.440
had to add a lot of additional
complex logic later on in order

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.510
to reassemble some of our data.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.070
Our log sources are split up.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.970
And sometimes a message
might come in now,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.710
and then the remainder
of that message

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.290
might come 20 minutes
later, a couple hours later,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.310
or tomorrow.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.260
And when messages
aren't ordered,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.540
we don't really know when
something is necessarily

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.460
tied into another message.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.030
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Tell me a little bit

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.080
about what's coming next.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.610
What new evolutions
are you excited

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.510
about for this pipeline?

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.427
NOAM DOROGOYER: Right.

00:02:58.427 --> 00:03:00.640
So we have three approaches.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.150
So firstly, we want to expand
on the machine learning

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:03.900
algorithms.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.805
We have a supervised and
unsupervised application

00:03:06.805 --> 00:03:08.430
that is essentially
in alpha right now.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.840
We want to finish
it, and we want

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.760
to have it outputting
meaningful results.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.170
Then they want to
start spreading

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.030
our pipeline to other cities.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.210
We want to maybe write some
white papers or some research

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:23.430
topics, and have them create
a similar secure space that we

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.280
have.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.210
And finally, we want
to expand to sim space.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:30.240
Right now, we're creating a
simulation environment that

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.760
is nearly identical to our own.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.930
And we can use that to
gather training data

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.150
and model some of
the attacks that are

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.770
coming against city systems.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.060
MAX SALTONSTALL: It's great
to know that you and your team

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.380
are working hard to
keep us all safe.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.310
Thank you very much.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.928
NOAM DOROGOYER: Thank you.

00:03:45.928 --> 00:03:47.720
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Interested in checking out

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.770
Apache Beam and Data Flow?

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.567
Get started with some templates
to get up and running quickly.

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:54.400
Thanks for watching.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.650
And make sure to subscribe
for more great Google Cloud

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:57.600
Platform content.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.060
We'll see you on the next
episode of "Stack Chat."

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

